\begin{abstract}
Randomised cryptographic arguments are routinely phrased in the language of
probability, but in the finite regime where they are actually instantiated they are
statements about the \emph{fraction of a finite seed space} on which an event
occurs. We develop this bookkeeping layer exactly: for a finite seed space $\Omega$
and a decidable event $A$ we study the rational-valued functional
$\fr_\Omega(A)=|\{s\in\Omega: A(s)\}|/|\Omega|$, establish its measure-like calculus
together with the precise non-emptiness guards under which each normalisation law
holds, and prove the structural result on which everything else rests: the level
sets of a cost function bounded by $B$ partition the seed space, so their fractions
sum to $1$. From this single identity we derive the sublevel partial-sum identity,
Markov's inequality in level-set form, and both layer-cake identities for the
average cost. We then instantiate the calculus in three settings. (i) \emph{Bounded
witness search}: we prove the guarding facts and an exactness (``honesty'')
statement, show that Markov's inequality applied to a bounded search yields the
bound $B/t$, which is provably vacuous on its entire range, and replace it with a
distribution-sensitive \emph{first-probe savings} bound
$\mathbb{E}[\cost]\le B-(B-1)p$; we also prove that $k$-fold amplification is the
exact identity $1-(1-\varepsilon)^k$. (ii) \emph{Sampled monitoring}: a guarded,
periodically healed system under constant attack is compromised at time $n\ge 1$
exactly when $n$ is not a checkpoint, whence the compromised fraction of the window
$(0,N]$ equals $\frac{k-1}{k}+\frac{N\bmod k}{kN}$; the folklore value $(k-1)/k$ is a
strict lower bound attained if and only if $k\mid N$, with a uniform $1/N$ envelope,
and a dichotomy holds: period $k=1$ gives $0$ while every $k\ge 2$ gives at least
$1/2$. (iii) \emph{Rewinding}: the global accepting fraction of a two-move protocol
is the average of its row fractions; an accepting fraction strictly above $1/|C|$
forces a randomness accepting two distinct challenges, and this threshold is sharp;
and a general heavy-row splitting lemma states that for every $\alpha>0$ at least a
$(1-\alpha)e$ fraction of rows are $\alpha e$-heavy, giving a quantitative rewinding
theorem.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} seed fraction, level set, layer cake, bounded search,
rewinding, special soundness, sampled monitoring, amplification.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Probability, or counting?}

A typical claim in randomised cryptography reads: \emph{with probability at least
$\varepsilon$ over the seed, the adversary is caught / the protocol accepts / the
extractor succeeds.} When the scheme is instantiated, the seed ranges over a finite
set: keys of a fixed length, challenges from a fixed set, a fixed number of coin
flips. The claim is then a statement about the cardinality of a subset of a finite
set, divided by the cardinality of that set. It is arithmetic in $\mathbb{Q}$.

Treating it as arithmetic rather than as measure theory has three benefits. First,
every statement becomes \emph{exact}: one obtains identities where the probabilistic
idiom offers estimates. Second, every statement becomes \emph{decidable} on concrete
instances: a claimed bound can be checked by exhaustive enumeration on a small seed
space, which is an unusually effective way to find errors. Third---and this is the
recurring theme of the paper---the degenerate cases become visible. A fraction with
denominator $0$ is not a probability; it is a number that the convention $x/0=0$
silently sets to zero, and any normalisation claim about it is false.

\subsection{What is developed here}

Section~\ref{sec:calculus} develops the functional $\fr_\Omega$ and the
\emph{level-set decomposition}: a bounded cost function stratifies the seed space,
and the fractions of the strata sum to $1$. This one identity yields the sublevel
identity, Markov's inequality, and both layer-cake formulas for the average cost.
The applications are bounded witness search stratified by probe count
(Section~\ref{sec:search}), the compromised fraction of a sampled-monitoring run
(Section~\ref{sec:monitor}), and heavy rows, the rewinding threshold and knowledge
extraction (Section~\ref{sec:rewind}). Section~\ref{sec:algos} collects the
algorithmic content, Section~\ref{sec:disc} discusses the negative results, and
Section~\ref{sec:future} lists open directions.

